## Component Diagram

Static view: which top-level component under `src/{{ python_package_name }}/` depends on which.
One box per component, an arrow `A ──▶ B` wherever `A` imports `B`; external services stay in
the System Overview above. This is the diagram `.importlinter`'s contracts should agree with — a
forbidden import is an arrow that must not exist here.

`just ci-arch` checks that every top-level component is named inside this section's fenced block,
so a new component cannot land without a box. It does not check the arrows; `/arch-review` does.

```
<!-- e.g.
   ┌────────┐      ┌────────┐      ┌────────┐
   │  cli   │ ───▶ │ core   │ ───▶ │ store  │
   └────────┘      └────────┘      └────────┘
-->
```

## Components

Each top-level module or package directly under `src/{{ python_package_name }}/` gets a `##`
section below: what it's for, its public interface, what it depends on, and — if its internal
flow isn't obvious from the name — a small ASCII diagram of that flow (the Component Diagram
above shows how components relate; this one shows what happens inside one). `just ci-arch` only checks that the
section exists (mechanical coverage); it does not verify the content still matches the code. Run
the `/arch-review` skill (`.agents/skills/arch-review/SKILL.md`) periodically, or after a PR that
changes a documented component's behavior, to catch drift a coverage check can't.

